INT. CITY HOSPITAL - EMERGENCY ROOM BAY 4 - NIGHT

Curtains, beeping monitors, a gurney. A bag of saline drips. A young man lies on the bed, pale, one arm limp.

DR. FELICITY ABARA-LINDGREN (40s, scrubs, tired eyes) checks his pulse. A tall man in an ill-fitting suit hovers at the curtain: the patient's brother.

FELICITY
He's stable. For now.

BROTHER
Thank God.

FELICITY
Don't thank anyone yet. Is there any history of heart trouble in your family?

BROTHER
I don't know. We weren't close.

FELICITY
Your name?

BROTHER
Anselmo.

FELICITY
Anselmo, I need the truth. What did he take?

ANSELMO
Nothing.

FELICITY
His blood says otherwise.

The monitor beeps faster. Felicity glances at it, then at the brother, and her tone drops.

FELICITY (CONT'D)
His pressure's falling. Nurse!

The brother steps in, grabs her sleeve.

ANSELMO
He took my father's pills. All of them. I watched him.

FELICITY
Why didn't you stop him?

ANSELMO
Because I wanted to see if he'd really do it.

She stares at him. Slowly, she pulls her sleeve free. The monitor settles. Her voice goes steady.

FELICITY
He's going to live, Mr. Anselmo. And you are going to sit down and think about what you just said.

He sits. The curtain swings shut.
